# 3 Setting up, running and restarting a case {#ch:setup}

## A program skeleton

Most Navier--Stokes programs have the same shape. This one is a complete,
compilable template; the worked examples in [chapter 5](05_examples) fill it in.

~~~literatec
#include "navier-stokes/centered.h"        // grid: quadtree by default
#include "two-phase.h"                     // only for two fluids
#include "navier-stokes/conserving.h"      //   ... with a large density ratio
#include "reduced.h"                       //   ... with gravity

#define LEVEL 8

// boundary conditions (defaults: free-slip walls everywhere)
u.n[left]  = dirichlet (1.);
p[left]    = neumann (0.);
pf[left]   = neumann (0.);
u.n[right] = neumann (0.);
p[right]   = dirichlet (0.);
pf[right]  = dirichlet (0.);

int main()
{
  size (1.);                  // domain side L0
  origin (0., 0.);            // lower-left corner
  init_grid (1 << LEVEL);     // 256 x 256 to start
  rho1 = 1000., mu1 = 1e-3;   // fluid 1 (f = 1)
  rho2 = 1.2,   mu2 = 1.8e-5; // fluid 2 (f = 0)
  G.y = -9.81;
  run();
}

event init (t = 0)
{
  fraction (f, 0.3 - y);      // fluid 1 below y = 0.3
}

event logfile (i++)
  fprintf (stderr, "

event adapt (i++)
  adapt_wavelet ({f, u}, (double[]){1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-2}, LEVEL);

event end (t = 2.)
{
  dump ("final");
}
~~~

## Choosing the grid

| Grid | Use |
|:---|:---|
| `grid/cartesian.h` | uniform, simplest; no multigrid hierarchy, so slow
Poisson solves; for tests |
| `grid/multigrid.h` (`multigrid3D.h`) | uniform with a multigrid
hierarchy; fast; allows non-square boxes with `dimensions(nx = 4)` |
| `grid/quadtree.h`, `grid/octree.h` | adaptive; the default; the
domain is always a square or cube |
| `grid/bitree.h`, `multigrid1D.h` | one-dimensional problems |

On an adaptive tree the domain is one cube of side `L0`: `size()`
sets it and `origin()` only moves it. To model a long channel, either
accept the full cube and keep the unused part coarse with adaptation
([section 9.5](09_grid#sec:wavelet)), or use `multigrid.h` with `dimensions()` if a
uniform grid is affordable. `mask()` can cut parts of a tree domain
away, but the source notes that it does not work together with MPI in some
output functions.

## Resolution

`init_grid(n)` creates a uniform grid of $n$ cells per side; $n$ must
be a power of two on trees and multigrids. For an adaptive run, start with a
coarse or moderate grid and let `adapt_wavelet()` refine, or refine
the initial grid where you know detail is needed:

~~~literatec
refine (sq(x) + sq(y) < sq(0.2) && level < LEVEL);   // around a body at the origin
~~~

The level sets the cell size, $\Delta= L_0/2^{\ell}$. Count cells across the
smallest feature you need (a body diameter, a wave height, a bubble): 10
cells give a rough answer, 20--30 a usable one, and a convergence study
(levels $\ell$, $\ell+1$, $\ell+2$) tells you whether the answer has
settled.

## Initial conditions

Initial conditions go in an `init` event (`t = 0`):

- a volume fraction from a level-set function, positive inside fluid 1:
  `fraction (f, 0.3 - y);`

- a solid body from a level-set function, positive in the fluid:
  `solid (cs, fs, sqrt(sq(x) + sq(y)) - R);`

- any field by a loop: `foreach() u.x[] = U0*f[];`

Constructive solid geometry combines level sets with `union()`
(`max`), `intersection()` (`min`) and `difference()`.

## Fluid properties and solver parameters

For one fluid, the viscosity is a face field `mu`; its default is zero
(inviscid). Set it in `main()` for a constant value, or in a
`properties` event if it varies:

~~~literatec
face vector muv[];
int main() { mu = muv; ... }
event properties (i++) {
  foreach_face()
    muv.x[] = fm.x[]*nu;      // always multiply by fm (solid fraction / metric)
}
~~~

For two fluids, `two-phase.h` computes $\rho$ and $\mu$ from
`rho1`, `rho2`, `mu1`, `mu2` and $f$. Surface tension
is `f.sigma`; gravity with `reduced.h` is `G` (and the
reference level `Z`).

The parameters you are most likely to change: `DT` (maximum time
step), `CFL`, `TOLERANCE` (lower it, for example to $10^{-4}$, if
accuracy of pressure matters), and `NITERMAX`.

## Adaptive refinement

~~~literatec
event adapt (i++)
  adapt_wavelet ({f, u}, (double[]){1e-3, 1e-2, 1e-2}, maxlevel, minlevel);
~~~

The list gives the fields to watch, and the array one tolerance per scalar
(a vector counts as its components). The tolerance is in the units of the
field: $10^{-2}$ for a velocity of order 1 means "refine where the
interpolation error exceeds 1% of the velocity scale". Volume fractions
use $10^{-3}$ to $10^{-2}$. With an embedded body, include `cs` so the
surface stays at the finest level. [Section 9.5](09_grid#sec:wavelet) explains the error
estimate.

## Running

Run in the background and keep the log:

~~~bash
OMP_NUM_THREADS=8 ./case > out.log 2> log.txt &
tail -f log.txt
~~~

Print at least $t$, `dt`, the cell count and `mgp.i` every few
steps. A time step that keeps shrinking, or a pressure solve that needs
many cycles, is the first sign of trouble ([chapter 6](06_pitfalls)).

**Cost.** The cost is roughly the number of cells times the number of
steps. Halving $\Delta$ doubles the number of steps (CFL) and multiplies the
cells by 4 in 2D and 8 in 3D, so one more level costs about 8 times more in
2D and 16 times more in 3D. Test changes at a low level and short end time
first.

## Dumping and restarting

`dump()` writes all fields to a binary file; `restore()` reads one
back. The usual restart pattern tries to restore in `init` and only
sets the initial condition if there was nothing to restore:

~~~literatec
event init (t = 0)
{
  if (!restore (file = "restart")) {
    fraction (f, 0.3 - y);         // fresh start
  }
}

event snapshots (t += 0.5)
{
  char name[80];
  sprintf (name, "dump-
  dump (name);
}
~~~

Copy the dump you want to continue from to `restart`. Two things are
not in a dump: face fields such as the embedded face fractions `fs`
(rebuild the solid with `solid()` after restoring), and the pressure
(`p.nodump` is set), which the first projection recomputes. Dump files
grow quickly with the level; delete old ones.

